Z||000000||pontiacs4me||02-16-2001||02:46 AM||||Hi, <BR>Im new to the fiero game.  Ive always wanted one, so today I bought 2.  I also own a 1973 formula firebird.  But this is a fiero fourm so heres about them, one is a 1985 2m6 and one is a 1987 standard model.  the engine in the 87 is seized up and the chassis on the 85 is messed up so I am putting the 2.8 liter in the 87.  Eventually I want to put a 3800 supercharged v6 in there.  but for now I just need to get the 2.8 liter running.  Will I have to swap the computers or anything like that?  What all will I need from the 85?  any info would be apreciated.  Email me directly if you would like.  thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||MontanaFiero||02-16-2001||03:41 AM||||Welcome to the Forum, and Welcome to the Excitement of Fieros!<P>To answer a couple of your questions, yes, you will have to swap computers and engine harnesses. But that's the easy part. Do both cars have manual transmissions (or auto)? You are probably going to find the job a whole lot easier if you swap the whole engine cradle with tranny and engine attached.<P><P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- project &lt;&lt;||||reg||10
Z||000002||Carrolles||02-16-2001||07:23 AM||||The v-6 has a higher pressure fuel pump than the 4 cyl.  Also the fuel lines are routed differently on the two engines.<P>If they are both auto trans I believe you will be ok.  If they are standard trans then it is easiest to keep the trans that came in the car since the shift cables would be different.  The A/C compressors and hoses are different.<P>Also, I believe the cruise control in the 87 is run by the ecm.  The 85 cruise control has its own separate control box.<P>As was mentioned you'll need the ecm (computer) and rear wiring harness from the v-6 car to stay with the v-6 engine.<P>I believe dropping the cradles from both is the best way to go.  I have never pulled a Fiero engine from the top.<P>While you have the cradles out it is a good time to pull the v-6 exhaust manifolds to weld and port them, its not hard to do.<P>Keep us posted on your progress.<P>Good luck and welcome to the forum.<P>Carroll<P>p.s.  Here is an article that might help.<BR> <A HREF="http://msnhomepages.talkcity.com/RallyRd/scca_fiero/4cylindertoV6.html" TARGET=_blank>http://msnhomepages.talkcity.com/RallyRd/scca_fiero/4cylindertoV6.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 02-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||02-16-2001||08:20 AM||||Hi Guy<BR>See you joined up. You'll find a wealth of info here. See ya on PT.<P>Hey people notice that he's swapping a V6 to a V6 so problems should be  minimum.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_1.gif">  <BR>SC 3800||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||02-16-2001||11:19 AM||||phil, he's taking the 2.8 from the 85 and putting it into the 87 standard model (i'm assuming he's talking about a coupe) so that's changing a 2.5 for the 2.8 v6.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
