Z||000011||pontiacs4me||02-17-2001||04:39 AM||||I have another problem now.  I tried to fire up the 2.8 today and the fuel pump doesnt kick on when I turn on the key.  I checked out all the fuel pump relays and tried jumping them, I hear a clicking sound about 1/2 second after I turn on the key though.  I tried everything and cant get any fuel pressure.  I checked all the fuses it has plenty of gas, I dont know what to do next.  I planned on dropping the tank, and draining all the gas and starting from there to see If I can get it going.  Is it possible that the pump can freeze up?  It gets about 10 degrees here at night, the guy I bought it from told me he had a new tank and pump put in about 6 months ago and it cost him 600 dollars because the pump isnt replaceable alone, you have to buy it with the tank as a unit.  is this true?  Would it be cheaper to put an external electric fuel pump inline on the fuel line, if it is a fuel pump problem?  thats alot of money for a fuel pump.  anyone have any ideas?  thanks||||reg||1
Z||000012||MontanaFiero||02-17-2001||05:11 AM||||First of all it's 6 degrees <I>below</I> zero here right now and I drive my car every day and it starts right up every time (knock on wood)<P>Second, I just did a "curb-side" fuel pump change for about $60. The pump is available separately at almost any auto  parts store. <BR>If the pump dies twice like that, then chances are there is a reason. There may  be dirt in the bottom of your tank that didn't get cleaned out the first time the pump was changed. The dirt may have caused the first pump to die too.<BR>&gt;&gt; my advice is to check the fuse, then swap the two relays to make sure it isn't the relay causing the problem. Then, drop the tank and pull the pump/sender assembly out and flush the tank. discard all of the old fuel (properly), and replace the fuel pump.<BR>&gt;this is a fairly easy job, just use a manual. The pump I had came with good instructions too.<P><BR>good luck<P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- project &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000013||MontanaFiero||02-17-2001||03:40 PM||||First of all it's 6 degrees <I>below</I> zero here right now and I drive my car every day and it starts right up every time (knock on wood)<P>Second, I just did a "curb-side" fuel pump change for about $60. The pump is available separately at almost any auto  parts store. <BR>If the pump dies twice like that, then chances are there is a reason. There may  be dirt in the bottom of your tank that didn't get cleaned out the first time the pump was changed. The dirt may have caused the first pump to die too.<BR>&gt;&gt; my advice is to check the fuse, then swap the two relays to make sure it isn't the relay causing the problem. Then, drop the tank and pull the pump/sender assembly out and flush the tank. discard all of the old fuel (properly), and replace the fuel pump.<BR>&gt;this is a fairly easy job, just use a manual. The pump I had came with good instructions too.<P><BR>good luck<P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- project &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
